      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.137,
            0.046,
            0.347,
            0.063
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 8"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.088,
            0.335,
            0.106
          ],
          "content": "In ∆AOB, we have,"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.106,
            0.591,
            0.126
          ],
          "content": "$a+a+60=180$ (interior angles of a triangle)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.125,
            0.344,
            0.143
          ],
          "content": "Therefore $2 a = 1 2 0 .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.144,
            0.272,
            0.161
          ],
          "content": "Thus $a = 6 0 .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.172,
            0.689,
            0.193
          ],
          "content": "Similarly, we can find the values of all the other angles."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.395,
            0.2,
            0.582,
            0.389
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_4_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.391,
            0.706,
            0.437
          ],
          "content": "Can we now identify what type of quadrilateral ABCD is? Notice that its angles all add up to $90^{\\circ} \\left( 30^{\\circ} + 60^{\\circ} \\right)$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.438,
            0.475,
            0.461
          ],
          "content": "What can we say about its sides?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.259,
            0.465,
            0.437,
            0.661
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.438,
                0.464,
                0.453,
                0.478
              ],
              "content": "B"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_4_image_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.492,
            0.462,
            0.689,
            0.659
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_4_image_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.666,
            0.848,
            0.741
          ],
          "content": "We can see that $\\Delta  AOB  \\cong \\Delta  COD$ and $\\Delta AOD \\cong \\Delta COB$ . Hence, $\\mathrm{AB} = \\mathrm{CD}$ , and $\\mathrm{AD} = \\mathrm{CB},$ since they are corresponding parts of congruent triangles. Therefore, ABCD is a rectangle since it satisfies the definition of a rectangle."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.782,
            0.685,
            0.847
          ],
          "content": "Will ABCD remain a rectangle if the angles between the diagonals are changed? Can we generalise this? Take one of the angles between the diagonals as x."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.693,
            0.746,
            0.877,
            0.924
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
